  "Question": "What is the reciprocal of \\[\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{2}{3}?\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{6}{7}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{6}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "First add the fractions using a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac23=\\frac36+\\frac46=\\frac76.\n\\]\nThe sum is nonzero. Its reciprocal is the number that multiplies it to give \\(1\\), so interchange numerator and denominator to obtain \\(\\boxed{\\frac67}\\), choice \\(\\text{A}\\).",
